  "text": "Here's the complete implementation of the `remove_adjacent_duplicates` function that takes a list of integers and returns a new list with consecutive duplicates removed, i.e., only the first of a group of consecutive duplicates is kept.\n\n```python\nimport unittest\n\ndef remove_adjacent_duplicates(lst):\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n    \n    result = [lst[0]]\n    for num in lst[1:]:\n        if num != result[-1]:\n            result.append(num)\n    return result\n\nclass TestRemoveAdjacentDuplicates(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_basic_functionality(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4]), [1, 2, 3, 4])\n    \n    def test_repeated_elements(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([5, 5, 5, 6, 7, 7]), [5, 6, 7])\n    \n    def test_multiple_duplicates(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 3, 4, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5]), [1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    unittest.main()\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- The function starts by checking if the list is empty and returns an empty list if it is.\n- It initializes a result list with the first element of the input list.\n- Then, it iterates over the remaining elements of the list. If the current number is different from the last element in the result list, it adds it to the result.\n- This effectively removes all consecutive duplicates, keeping only the first occurrence of a repeated sequence.\n\n### Example:\nFor input `[1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4]`, the output will be `[1, 2, 3, 4]`.",
